Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> The Lojban formal grammar is (or "can be", depending on your attitude
> to the PEG grammar) well defined. The particular parser
> implementations of that grammar are not unique, there are all sorts of
> different things you may want to do with an implementation, and there
> is really no reason to pick one implementation over another. Different
> uses will make use of different implementations, all based on the same
> grammar.

Yes and no.  There can be only one correct output parse, however the=20
parser gets to it (allowing that formatting differences in the output=20
aren't different parses), or the language is not syntactically=20
unambiguous.  If the language is defined to extend into multiple units=20
called "text", the boundaries between "text"s need to be unambiguous,=20
and only a parser that accurate reflects those boundaries is "correct".

Right now, there are no formal rules governing multiple texts and their=20
possible interactions.  Perhaps there should be, but I am inclined to=20
think we should wait till the BYFY finishes the simpler "single text"=20
problem we've been stuck on for years before making the job harder.  In=20
such a case, discussing this problem at all right now is out of order.

That being said, a new speaker may wish to explicitly append onto=20
another speaker's text.  Such a problem seems to lie within the=20
"single-text" grammar, though other interpretations are probably=20
possible.  If it is, then any specific method for doing so that is not=20
already part of the language should be documented, although it should be=20
considered a change if it is not currently part of the language (and=20
therefore put off until the current language is documented).=20
Experimental cmavo of course do not need to be considered by BYFY.

